From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: Sat, 18 Jun 1994 22:44:00 -0500
Subject: [#94-6-66] digital cash and free banking

Is there really any relationship? Neither is necessary to achieve the
other, nor sufficient. Remember, free banking does not imply a gold
standard, just an end to the central bank. And nothing would prevent a
regulated/central bank from issuing digital cash, though the regs would
have to change.

There is perhaps some synergy... If digital cash became the generally
accepted exchange medium in a massive black market economy, that would
grossly undermine the fed's power over the money supply, and possibly the
relevance of the money supply (if digicash is denominated in some other
unit, though that seems unlikely). And a less-regulated environment might
help digicash get off the ground.

Speaking of which, what would get digicash off the ground? Who wants it
badly enough to pay a premium for it (assuming early adopters always pay a
premium)?

Like White, I doubt the feds will consider anonymous digicash legal or
change regs to make it so, unless it is so overwhelmingly successful in the
black market that it makes other money irrelevant. Come to think of it, I'm
sure the FBI would lobby against regular cash if it hadn't existed
previously to checks, credit cards, and other data trailers.

