From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Sun, 19 Jun 94 13:40:52 GMT
Subject: [#94-6-70] digital cash and free banking

In re whether people are willing to pay a premium to use digital cash:
At present, I see digital cash as having one current and one potential
advantage over ordinary money: untracability and convenience.

The privacy is obviously most important for people in illegal lines
of work--digital cash might get established first in that part of
the economy, but the advantages of not paying taxes should become
obvious once there are enough people using the stuff. I imagine
digicash being used first in the mundane economy for such things
as under-the-table plumbing, and whole businesses that get most
or all of their income in digicash coming later.

As for convenience, I'm not sure if digicash has tremendous advantages
over credit cards--it seems as though what people mostly want from
credit cards (and are willing to pay quite a bit for) is credit--
a debit card would give equal convenience without the interest
charges, but not very many people use debit cards. 

Does anyone have any thought about how a transition from digicash
based on fiat money to an independent digicash system would work?
I'm assuming that the federal government will be weakened enough
that such a transition is very likely.

Nancy Lebovitz



