From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Sun, 19 Jun 1994 08:47:38 -0800
Subject: [#94-6-72] digital cash and free banking

There are those who say the central bank (Federal Reserve in the US) is
irrevelant anyway; with or without digital cash. Even some in the Federal
Reserve banks say this, and the calculations based on M2 and M3 sort of
bear this out.

I think it is a good idea to create memes for digital cash that don't
revolve around the legal/illegal theme. So here are some possible
advantages to digital cash that don't evoke it's seamy side.

1. Fixed value. Digital cash could be defined to have no inflation or
deflation. Fixed value becomes even more valuable for international
transactions by elliminating exchange rate risk.

2. Low latency. Earn interrest from the second of the transaction. (This
would not be an advantage to those who currently profit from the float and
I see this as one of the big impedimants to digital cash.)

3. Security. Digital cash can be made hard to steal or to use if stolen.

4. Credit. If people and businesses could in effect issue their own
currency and it's value be determined by others' perception of it's value
(like corporate credit but with less overhead) I think we would get much
better loans made and less credit risk overall.  Today it would reveal too
much information about ourselves to get a credit rating from everyone we
wish to do business with, plus it would be time consuming. 

5. Tax collection. Huh? Yes, tax collection. Say your digital cash
card/computer had a resident tax agent program running inside. Each
transaction would be monitored by the program and at the end of the quarter
the tax agent program would issue a figure signed by it's own digital
signature. That's all the info you give the IRS; the amount and the
signature. Even your identity could be kept secret if digital cash was used
on a wide enough scale. (I'll explain this later if anyone is interrested.)
It would be incumbant upon the taxing authorities to write tax agent
programs  popular enough to gain credibility in the marketplace. 

More on this concept in _Achieving Electronic Privacy_ by David Chaum, in
Scientific American August 1992. (I can't recomend this article highly
enough. Please read it if you haven't already)



