Actually, with current regulations, IMHO it's quite likely for digicash
to be denominated in something other than dollars. Most of the schemes
I've seen discussed are along the lines of the local barter or
labor-trading schemes that exist in some places, except that they're
global rather than being restricted to a small geographical area (e.g.
'You write me some software and I'll send you some digicash to pay for
your account on my server'). In this case the denomation would be
something like 'access-months' - that does give you an effective exchange 
rate, but it could well be variable rather than fixed.

> Speaking of which, what would get digicash off the ground? Who wants it
> badly enough to pay a premium for it (assuming early adopters always pay a
> premium)?

Me for one, and anyone else who occasionally needs to pay small amounts
of money to people in foreign countries (I'm currently in the UK,
BTW).  That's Ok for people who can accept credit cards (aside from the
obvious fraud possibilities, and the privacy concerns that digicash
addresses), but normally if I want to pay someone, say, $ 17.25, I have
to go to the bank and get the minimum that they'll sell me, $ 100
(5x20) in travellers checks, mail a $ 20 check, and either forget about
the $ 2.75 extra, try to find something else they can sell me that I
probably don't really want, or get them to credit me somehow against
future purchases (and if I'm buying a very rare and obscure comic from
someone's advert on Usenet, that's not terribly likely even if I trust
them).

So already, I'm paying a high premium with about $ 4 in handling
charges, 1.5-2.5% on top of that for bank commission, plus I get a bad
exchange rate, then the excess that gets lost along the way (plus
perhaps $ 1.00-2.00 for postage). At that rate, even a digicash system
that charged me a 25% premium would often be cheaper, and for small
amounts (a dollar or three), even a 50% premium would be better.
International payments are expensive, especially from backward
countries like the UK, unless there's a lot of money involved and/or
you do it on a regular basis.

[ Yes, there are alternatives like getting a US $ bank account, or
  international money orders and the like, but the former would cost
  me more in bank charges and doesn't help with sending money to
  anywhere other than the US, and the latter involves finding a bank
  that actually sells them (especially for countries other than the US
  or those in the EC), paying the fees (about $ 10 last time I checked), and 
  getting the person at the other end to go through the hassle of cashing
  them rather than just paying travllers checks straight into their account.
  The extra inconvenience doesn't usually make up for the sometimes 
  slightly lower cost. ]

There are lots of occasions where I see adverts for stuff for sale in
other countries that I'd be interested in buying, but when the full
cost of doing so is added up it just becomes too expensive to justify.
Internet commerce is desperately in need of even a fairly expensive
digicash system to solve this. I certainly think that a system which
initially charged a 10% premium would be quite profitable and widely
used, at least for international purchases, and a system that charged
merchants less than the credit card fee (what is that, 1-4 % ?) would
definitely be welcomed. Personally, I'd pay 5% just for the anonymity,
but I'm not sure how many other people would.

> Like White, I doubt the feds will consider anonymous digicash legal or
> change regs to make it so, unless it is so overwhelmingly successful in the
> black market that it makes other money irrelevant. Come to think of it, I'm
> sure the FBI would lobby against regular cash if it hadn't existed
> previously to checks, credit cards, and other data trailers.

Well, of course they would. The anonymity of cash is a yet another big
problem for Big Brother. Banking regulations are just another set of
outdated laws that we need to get rid of.

			Mark

